// Where an outcome becomes four columns. The library above is spliced
// in whole and holds the whole decision: handed the counters and stamps
// the row carries now and how this pass ended, it answers the
// consecutive_failures, last_success_at, last_failure_at and flagged
// values the row should carry next. Nothing below states a rule of its
// own. The threshold, the reset to a real zero, the stamp that does not
// move and the flag that is set here and never cleared here are settled
// in src/lib/source-health.ts and under test beside it.
//
// What IS written here is the outcome, because no module holds it. The
// library takes a succeeded boolean and does not work one out: it never
// sees a document, and what counts as a good pass is a fact about the
// readings this pass produced. tests/sources/fail-flag-keep.test.ts
// composes the same answer over the two adapters under src/sources/,
// and this node matches it.
//
// THE RULE. A pass succeeded when it produced at least one reading and
// every reading it produced is one the contract accepted. Two halves,
// and each is doing work.
//
// A SOURCE THAT ANSWERED AND DIVERGED FAILED. The payload arrived, so
// nothing threw and nothing timed out, and a health rule keyed on
// retrieval would call that working for as long as it kept rotting. A
// divergence says the opposite: the source answered in a shape its own
// contract does not describe, which is what an adapter going bad looks
// like from here and is the one signal a pass leaves behind. The
// reading is kept either way, with the reason it was refused, so a flag
// raised over a run of them names something a person can go and read.
//
// A SOURCE THAT ANSWERED NOTHING NEW DID NOT. Every reading a pass
// makes may already be in the corpus, and Write Documents says so on
// each of its items. This node does not read that member, and reads
// nothing else off those items either. A page that has not changed
// since the last pass, a listing whose top is where it was, a feed
// nobody has posted to: that is what a healthy source looks like most
// of the time, and counting a repeat capture as a failure would flag
// every quiet source in the domain within the threshold's worth of
// passes, with nothing on this canvas able to clear a flag. What was
// fetched and what was new are two questions, and only the first is
// asked here.
//
// A PASS THAT PRODUCED NO READING AT ALL FAILED. That is the other half
// of the rule, and it covers three endings at once: a request that
// never completed, a parser_config nobody can read, and a payload
// holding no records where the config said records would be. The last
// of those is what a source whose shape has moved looks like before
// anybody notices. Parting it from a listing that is genuinely empty
// would mean walking the payload again here, which is the extraction
// written a second time, so the ambiguity is resolved toward counting
// and what that costs is said rather than hidden: a source quiet in
// that particular way climbs the counter.
//
// WHAT THIS NODE DOES NOT DECIDE. It does not read the HTTP status.
// That sits on Extract Records' items and is no part of the rule: a 404
// or a 500 arrives as data because neverError is on, and an error
// document is not a reading a contract written for the real thing
// accepts, so the contract reports it already. A status rule beside the
// contract would be a second judgment able to disagree with the first,
// and the one case it would add, an error page a loose contract happens
// to accept, is answered by naming a member in the contract that an
// error page does not carry, which is a row an operator edits.
//
// It does not clear a flag and holds no member that could. It does not
// touch enabled: the answer is four columns and the update below is
// built from exactly the members the answer holds, so a column named
// here is a column somebody eventually writes.
//
// And it writes nothing. The four values travel as members of an item
// and are columns only in Apply Source Health below.
//
// ONE MOMENT FOR THE PASS. The stamp is read once, at the top, and
// handed to every source, so the rows this pass writes agree about when
// it ran. That is the reason the library takes the moment as an
// argument rather than reading a clock it has no way to agree with, the
// clock here being the instance's and the one the update could have
// used being the database's. It goes into whichever of the two stamps
// moved and into nothing else.
//
// PAIRING. Sources are one row each and readings are one item each, so
// this node gathers where the nodes above pair: every item Extract
// Records emitted carries the source_id of the row it was read under,
// and the gathering is by that id. The id is copied and never
// re-derived, so whichever spelling the driver chose for a bigint is
// the same on both sides of the lookup.
//
// Refusals guard it rather than assumptions, every one of these being a
// mis-pairing nothing downstream undoes. The write group answers one
// item per reading, so its count and Extract Records' count are the
// same number or something dropped an item. A reading filed under a
// source this pass did not select says the pairing went wrong further
// up. And every selected source has at least one item gathered under
// it, Extract Records emitting one for a source that produced none, so
// a source with nothing under it fell out of the stream rather than
// failed: reading that as a failure would flag a source over an item
// nobody can find.
//
// CARDINALITY. This node answers one item per SOURCE where it was
// handed one per reading, which is where the stream stops being about
// documents. A node below reaching for a document reads it back off
// Write Documents by name, the same way this one reaches Extract
// Records.

// A moment in whatever shape the driver handed one back. The library
// above carries a stamp it is not moving straight through, so a Date
// read off the row would be a Date going back into a query parameter.
// Normalising here is the caller taking the choice the library says is
// the caller's to take: this one builds SQL and so holds strings.
//
// Anything that is neither a Date nor a string is passed on as it came
// and the cast in Apply Source Health refuses it there. That is louder
// than a null written in its place, which would claim the source has
// never once succeeded.
function stampOf(value) {
  return value instanceof Date
    ? value.toISOString()
    : value;
}

const ACCEPTED = 'ok';
const sources = $('Select Active Sources').all();
const readings = $('Extract Records').all();
const writes = $input.all();
const at = new Date().toISOString();

if (readings.length !== writes.length) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-ingest] Judge Source Health expects one write per reading: '
    + String(writes.length) + ' writes against ' + String(readings.length)
    + ' readings',
  );
}

// Allocated per run rather than held above, which is the same rule the
// spliced library obeys: nothing here outlives the call.
const gathered = new Map();

for (const source of sources) {
  gathered.set(source.json.source_id, []);
}

for (const reading of readings) {
  const under = gathered.get(reading.json.source_id);

  if (under === undefined) {
    throw new Error(
      '[ar-ingest] Judge Source Health gathered a reading under source '
      + String(reading.json.source_id)
      + ', which Select Active Sources did not answer',
    );
  }

  // Every item, judged or not. The two counts answer different halves
  // of the rule: how many items a source produced says whether it is in
  // the stream at all, and how many of them carry a status says whether
  // it read anything.
  under.push(reading.json.parse_status);
}

return sources.map((source) => {
  const row = source.json;
  const statuses = gathered.get(row.source_id);

  if (statuses.length === 0) {
    throw new Error(
      '[ar-ingest] Judge Source Health has nothing at all under source '
      + String(row.source_id)
      + ', which Extract Records answers an item for even when it read '
      + 'no record',
    );
  }

  const judged = statuses.filter((status) => status !== null);

  const next = sourceHealth(
    {
      consecutiveFailures: row.consecutive_failures,
      lastSuccessAt: stampOf(row.last_success_at),
      lastFailureAt: stampOf(row.last_failure_at),
      flagged: row.flagged,
    },
    {
      succeeded: judged.length > 0
        && judged.every((status) => status === ACCEPTED),
      at,
    },
  );

  return {
    json: {
      run_id: row.run_id,
      source_id: row.source_id,
      consecutive_failures: next.consecutiveFailures,
      last_success_at: next.lastSuccessAt,
      last_failure_at: next.lastFailureAt,
      flagged: next.flagged,
    },
  };
});

__INLINE:shingle.ts__
// Where a new document is held against the corpus it landed in. The
// library above is spliced in whole and holds the whole judgement: how
// a body becomes a sketch, when two sketches may be compared at all,
// how much they overlap and what overlap means one document arrived
// twice. Nothing below states a similarity rule of its own. The shingle
// width, the sketch size, the threshold and the refusal to judge a
// partial sketch are settled in src/lib/shingle.ts and under test
// beside it.
//
// What IS written here is the composition, because no module holds it.
// Which documents are sketched, which members each one is held against,
// and what a mark is: three questions the library cannot answer, every
// one of them about a pass rather than about a pair of bodies.
//
// NEW DOCUMENTS ONLY. Write Documents answers document_inserted on
// every item, and an item that did not insert is a repeat capture: the
// row was in the corpus before this pass reached it, and whatever was
// going to happen to it happened when it was written. Sketching it
// would hold it against a corpus it is already a member of, and passing
// it on would offer the group below a document to spend on twice. An
// item that carried no reading at all wrote no row and has no body, and
// falls out for the same reason.
//
// EARLIER MEMBERS ONLY. A member is compared only where its id is below
// the document's own, and that one rule does two jobs. Write Documents
// ran above this node, so this pass's rows are already in the window
// and a document would otherwise converge with itself at an overlap of
// one. And two near enough items captured in the same pass would each
// mark the other, both would be gated out below, and the pass would
// keep neither. Ordering on the id makes the mark antisymmetric: the
// earlier row keeps the pass and the later one carries the mark.
//
// MARKED, NEVER DROPPED. Every new document comes out of this node
// whether or not it converged with anything. The mark is a member on
// the item and the gate below is what acts on it, which keeps the
// decision in one place and leaves the reason for it readable beside
// the document it was made about. Dropping a document here would leave
// a row in the corpus that nothing on this canvas ever accounts for.
//
// WHAT IS NOT COMPARED, AND WHY THAT IS NOT A ZERO. The library answers
// an empty sketch for a body shorter than one shingle and refuses a
// comparison unless both sketches are full. That second rule is a
// correctness bound rather than tidiness: overlap is divided by the
// smaller of the two sketches, so a partial sketch inflates without
// limit and a short body reads as a perfect match for any longer one
// that contains it. A member the sketch could not be built from is left
// out of the comparison set entirely rather than compared and scored
// zero.
//
// NULL VERSUS ZERO, which that bound makes a live question on every
// item here. nearest_similarity is null when no comparison happened at
// all and a real number when one did, a measured overlap of zero
// included. compared is the count that says which of the two it was,
// and sketch_size says why when the count is zero. A document nothing
// could be compared against is not a document that is unlike
// everything.
//
// THE THRESHOLD IS THE LIBRARY'S. sketchesConverge is called with no
// threshold, so SHINGLE_THRESHOLD applies, and nothing on this canvas
// overrides it. A per domain threshold would be a member of
// domains.settings, and DomainSettings in src/db/schema/domains.ts
// declares none: adding one is a schema edit and an operator's decision
// rather than something to slip into a Code node as a read of a key
// nobody declared.
//
// PAIRING. Two lists are read back by name and paired by INDEX, which
// is the pairing Write Documents already rests on: it answers one item
// per reading whatever happened, so its items and Extract Records'
// items are the same list twice over. The body is on the reading and
// the row it landed in is on the write, and a document needs both.
// Select Active Sources is not read here at all, a source being no part
// of what makes two documents the same document.
//
// CARDINALITY. This node is handed one item, the corpus, and answers
// one per NEW DOCUMENT, which is where the stream stops being about
// sources and starts being about documents. Nothing below it pairs by
// index: every item carries its own document_id, which is the handle a
// node below has on the row and the only one it needs.
//
// And it writes nothing. No column holds a sketch and none holds a
// mark. Both live on the item, and the gate below is the only reader
// either of them has.

// The body a documents row holds, read the way Write Documents reads
// it: a member that is not a string is not text whatever it would
// coerce to, and the empty string is what a capture that yielded no
// text was stored as. The same reading serves a corpus entry, whose
// body came back off the column that clause wrote.
function bodyOf(record) {
  return record !== null
    && typeof record === 'object'
    && typeof record.body === 'string'
    ? record.body
    : '';
}

// An id as a number, for the one comparison here that asks which of two
// rows arrived first rather than whether two ids are the same. The two
// sides are spelled differently and neither spelling is wrong: a bigint
// column comes back off the driver as text, where an id inside a jsonb
// array was parsed out of JSON as a number. Text compares
// lexicographically, which would sort 99 after 100 and mark the wrong
// one of a pair.
//
// Every other id on this canvas is copied and compared for equality,
// which needs no reading at all. This one is the exception and says so.
function idOrder(value) {
  return Number(value);
}

const corpus = $input.first();
const readings = $('Extract Records').all();
const writes = $('Write Documents').all();

if (corpus === undefined) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-ingest] Mark Near Duplicates was handed no corpus item, which '
    + 'Select Recent Corpus answers whatever the domain holds',
  );
}

if (readings.length !== writes.length) {
  throw new Error(
    '[ar-ingest] Mark Near Duplicates pairs one write to one reading by '
    + 'index: ' + String(writes.length) + ' writes against '
    + String(readings.length) + ' readings',
  );
}

// Allocated per run rather than held above, which is the rule the
// spliced library obeys: nothing here outlives the call.
const members = [];
const held = Array.isArray(corpus.json.corpus)
  ? corpus.json.corpus
  : [];

for (const entry of held) {
  const order = idOrder(entry.id);
  const sketch = bodySketch(bodyOf(entry));

  // A member this node cannot place in time, or cannot sketch, is no
  // opinion rather than a comparison that answers nothing. Leaving it
  // out of the set is what keeps the compared count below a
  // measurement: a member counted and skipped would make a document
  // look compared against material nothing ever read.
  if (!Number.isFinite(order) || sketch.length === 0) {
    continue;
  }

  members.push({ order, id: entry.id, sketch });
}

const items = [];

for (const [index, write] of writes.entries()) {
  const row = write.json;

  // An item that carried no reading, which Write Documents answers a
  // null document_id for. There is no row and no body, and a fetch that
  // read nothing is the health group's subject rather than this one's.
  if (row.document_id === null || row.document_id === undefined) {
    continue;
  }

  // A driver that spelled the flag some other way would otherwise drop
  // every document in silence and leave the pass looking like one that
  // captured nothing new. The column is boolean and Write Documents
  // reads it out of an EXISTS, so anything else is worth stopping for.
  if (row.document_inserted !== true && row.document_inserted !== false) {
    throw new Error(
      '[ar-ingest] Mark Near Duplicates cannot read document_inserted on '
      + 'document ' + String(row.document_id)
      + ', which Write Documents answers as a boolean',
    );
  }

  if (!row.document_inserted) {
    continue;
  }

  const documentOrder = idOrder(row.document_id);

  if (!Number.isFinite(documentOrder)) {
    throw new Error(
      '[ar-ingest] Mark Near Duplicates cannot order document '
      + String(row.document_id)
      + ', which Write Documents answers as the id of the row a reading '
      + 'is in',
    );
  }

  const body = bodyOf(readings[index].json.record);
  const sketch = bodySketch(body);
  let compared = 0;
  let nearest = null;
  let converged = null;

  for (const member of members) {
    if (member.order >= documentOrder) {
      continue;
    }

    if (!sketchComparable(sketch, member.sketch)) {
      continue;
    }

    const similarity = sketchSimilarity(sketch, member.sketch);

    compared += 1;

    if (nearest === null || similarity > nearest) {
      nearest = similarity;
    }

    // The FIRST member it converges with, and the array arrived
    // ascending by id, so that is the earliest one. A later member
    // converging too changes nothing worth recording: a mark names one
    // row a person can go and read, not every row an item resembles.
    if (converged === null && sketchesConverge(sketch, member.sketch)) {
      converged = member.id;
    }
  }

  items.push({
    json: {
      run_id: row.run_id,
      domain_id: row.domain_id,
      source_id: row.source_id,
      document_id: row.document_id,
      body,
      sketch_size: sketch.length,
      compared,
      nearest_similarity: nearest,
      duplicate_of: converged,
    },
  });
}

return items;

__INLINE:static-gate.ts__
// Where a document becomes a decision, and the last thing that happens
// before anything is spent. The library above is spliced in whole and
// holds the whole gate: how a term set is read, what a match is worth,
// how the sum is compared against a threshold and what sentence is
// written beside the answer. Nothing below states a scoring rule of its
// own.
//
// THE THREE FIELDS. gate_score, gate_decision and gate_reason, and no
// column carries any of them. src/lib/static-gate.ts names them and
// says the phase that wires the gate into a workflow decides where they
// land: this is that phase, and they land on the item. The ceiling node
// below reads the decision, so a document the gate ignored costs a
// model call of zero. That is the whole of what running a free decision
// first buys, and it is why this node sits above that group rather than
// beside it.
//
// WHAT COUNTS AS A SURVIVOR, and the staging that decides it. A near
// duplicate is not one: Mark Near Duplicates above named the earlier
// row it converged with, and this node writes that mark out as an
// ignore before the library is called. The library's own contract is
// what makes that one call rather than two paths. A record whose
// decision field is not the pending value comes back untouched, so the
// duplicates are decided here, the rest are decided by the gate, and
// one list goes through applyStaticGate whatever is in it.
//
// THE TEXT SCORED IS THE STORED BODY, not a prepared chunk.
// src/lib/chunk.ts is the reduction a model node's input goes through
// and it belongs to the group below this one, which is the group this
// gate exists to keep a document out of. What scoring the whole body
// costs runs in one direction only: a term matching in navigation
// furniture or a footer raises a score, and a raised score parks a
// document for a person rather than spending anything on it. GateChunk
// is declared structurally for exactly this, its own comment naming the
// plain object a Code node hands across a connection.
//
// THE THRESHOLD IS THE LIBRARY'S. No threshold is passed, so
// DEFAULT_THRESHOLD applies. A per domain threshold would be a member
// of domains.settings, and DomainSettings in src/db/schema/domains.ts
// declares none: adding one is a schema edit and an operator's decision
// rather than a read of a key nobody declared. A domain that has
// written no terms therefore scores every document at zero and gates
// all of them out, which is the safe direction for a gate on spending
// and is visible rather than silent. The sentence written beside each
// one carries the score, the threshold and that no term matched.
//
// NO LANGUAGE OPINION, and none is available. A detector is a function
// the caller supplies and a spliced library imports nothing, so there
// is nothing on this canvas to supply one with. The library reads that
// as no reason to doubt the term set and scores every document against
// it as it stands, which is what a deployment with no detector did
// before there was one.
//
// THE REASON NEVER QUOTES THE DOCUMENT. The library's own explanation
// carries the operator's patterns and the arithmetic and nothing off
// the text, and the two sentences written here name a row id and a
// state. gate_reason is a column read at a glance, which is exactly
// where untrusted content is least likely to be noticed.
//
// CARDINALITY. One item in, one item out, and each carries what it
// arrived with plus the three fields. The term set is read back off
// Load Domain Context by name for the reason everything is: a Postgres
// node in the middle of this chain replaced the item that held it long
// before the stream reached here.

// Why a near duplicate was not scored. It names the row it converged
// with and takes nothing off either document, the similarity that
// decided it being a member on the item beside this sentence.
const DUPLICATE_REASON = 'near duplicate of document ';

// Why a document could not be gated at all. Write Documents stores a
// body for every reading and an empty one for a capture that yielded no
// text, so this is a document that was kept rather than one that is
// missing.
const EMPTY_BODY_REASON = 'the stored body is empty';

const terms = $('Load Domain Context').first().json.terms;

return $input.all().map((item) => {
  const row = item.json;
  const body = typeof row.body === 'string'
    ? row.body
    : '';
  const duplicate = row.duplicate_of;
  const staged = duplicate === null || duplicate === undefined
    ? { ...row, gate_decision: GATE_DECISION_PENDING }
    : {
      ...row,
      gate_score: null,
      gate_decision: GATE_DECISION_IGNORE,
      gate_reason: DUPLICATE_REASON + String(duplicate),
    };

  return {
    json: applyStaticGate(
      staged,
      {
        usable: body !== '',
        chunk: body,
        reason: body === ''
          ? EMPTY_BODY_REASON
          : '',
      },
      terms,
    ),
  };
});
